ging for russia but  in terms of the timing of an attack it  could be imminent which is why we're  advising all british citizens to leave  ukraine i understand whilst there is  still commercial routes open i  understand secretary my question is um  how quickly do we think that they could  move down to kiev if they didn't indeed  invade or very very quickly they they  have um  troops stationed around around ukraine  so there could be an attack on kiev uh  there could be an attack from the east  uh  and what we also suspect is there could  be a false flag operation  that there could be false claims that  that they have been provoked uh to give  a pretext uh for russian invasion and  what we have been doing is we've been  calling out the russians we've been  exposing uh their attempts to put a  puppet government into kiev we've been  exposing  their attempts to create a pretext but  what we do expect  over the next few days is there could be  an attempt  of a false flag operation to create a  pretext to claim the ukrainians are  attacking them so that the russians have  a justification  for invading ukraine is there any  evidence of that  there is evidence that that  that is the type of activity they're  looking at and we've seen the russians  do it before this is absolutely from the  russian playbook the use of cyber  attacks the attempts to undermine  the legitimacy of the ukrainian  government and the use of false flag  operations to justify a pretext for  invasion so those are the kinds of  things that we're looking out for  over the next day or so russian defense  minister says that's absolutely nonsense  and that many of the military drills  have already ended um and it looks as  though many of the troops will be moving  back from the borders within days we  can't take them at their word  well  why have they stationed a hundred  thousand troops on the ukrainian border  in a deliberately threatening fashion  and why is it the case that they have  the full supplies and preparations to be  able to mount an invasion and they  clearly are seeking to threaten ukraine  they're seeking to bully ukraine they've  been very clear that they have these  demands such as ukraine never being able  to join nato  such as moving ahead with the division  of parts of ukraine so it's very clear  that the russians have  some diplomatic aims which are  essentially reducing ukrainian  sovereignty and self-determination that  is why they've stationed those troops on  the border to seek to threaten nato and  threaten ukraine and that's why the only  thing i will believe kay is when they  move the troops away from the border  words are easy what they need to do is  move the troops away from the border do  we have any evidence that they've been  trying to end the crisis  well  they started the crisis first of all so  the only reason we are talking about  this today is because of russian  aggression everything nato has done has  been defensive we've been strengthening  the eastern flank because of the  increased aggression that we have seen  from russia and you know we have been  talking to russia i was in  moscow last week the prime minister has  spoken to president putin president  biden has spoken to putin so of course  we want a diplomatic solution but it  can't be at the expense of ukrainian  sovereignty the fact is that in 1994  russia signed up to the budapest  memorandum which agreed that in exchange  for giving up its nuclear weapons  ukraine would have sovereignty and  territorial integrity and russia are now  trying to undermine that but not any  diplomatic solution  has to respect the sovereignty of  ukraine but not to be members of nato uh  and as we know the ukrainians are very  keen to be members of nato uh which is  where the on pass  stands at the moment  ukraine is a sovereign country and it is  a decision for ukraine and nato  about whether or not  ukraine should be a member of nato it's  not a decision for russia russia should  not have a veto  over other countries security  arrangements and the big risk of course  if there is an invasion into ukraine uh  that would be hugely damaging for russia  and ukraine and it will further  undermine the stability of europe  president putin has  actively questioned why other countries  in eastern europe are members of nato as  well so this i fear would not stop at  ukraine this is an attack  on  the neighboring states of russia and  other east european countries and trying  to undermine the legitimacy of them  being part of nato but as you know  president putin's view is that nato  promised that it wouldn't move any  further east when we saw the breakup of  uh the soviet union there was no  agreement of that sort k there is no  agreement of that sort the agreements  i've talked about the budapest 1994  agreement uh the other agreements that  were signed as part of the osce the  security cooperation agreement  none of them mentioned that countries  could not be part of nato in fact  those agreements are very explicit that  countries should have responsibility for  their own security arrangements and they  should be free to make those  arrangements provided they don't  undermine other countries security and  the fact is  russia's security is not being  undermined nobody believes  that russia is under attack the issue  here is that russia is threatening  ukraine by stationing troops on its  border and you say potentially not stop  at ukraine what do you mean by that  what i mean is if we saw  an invasion into ukraine  there would be severe costs in terms of  a long-running conflict  we could see the undermining of security  more broadly in europe and we could also  see other aggressors around the world  see it as an opportunity  to  to expand their ambitions too so this is  a very dangerous moment for the world  this is of course about ukraine which is  an important sovereign nation but it's  also about the wider stability of europe  and it's about wider global stability  and the message that we give to  aggressors and we have to give the  message to vladimir putin that there can  be no reward for aggression there can be  no reward for lining up troops on  somebody else's border and threatening  to invade talk about other countries  which which countries were you talking  about  well i mean we are we're currently in  negotiations with iran  uh over stopping them acquiring a  nuclear weapon  in vienna uh we've seen  china uh increase its economic coercion  and flights over taiwan  we cannot give a message  that aggression gets rewards what are we  going to do about the british embassy  the u.s have temporarily relocated  theirs  we have moved some of our staff  to the west of ukraine  to levieve but we retain a presence in  kiev i'm going to continue with that we  are we are going to continue but of  course we are constantly looking at the  situation ukraine making sure that we  are providing a service for british  nationals at the same time as making  sure our staff are kept safe if  necessary how many uh ukrainian refugees  should we accept  well we're not uh we're not at that  point  at the moment what we are doing is we  are working on humanitarian response  with partners with non-government  organizations and with other countries  and our priority at the moment  is securing the return of british  nationals who want to return there are  some british nationals who want to stay  in ukraine we've made it very clear to  them what the risks are but our priority  is securing the return of british  nationals how difficult is it to  negotiate with the russians they weren't  particularly polite to you when you went  last week lavrov uh being incredibly  rude saying it was a conversation  between a dumb and a deaf person it  seems like we listen but don't hear what  would you say in response to him  well i made it very clear to minister  lavrov and i was also delivering a  message to the russian people that if  there was an in